+Reading from a file
+~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+
+Use :func:`~rich.progress.open` to read a file while showing a progress bar. The bar advances by the number of bytes read, and the default total is the size of the file in bytes. The default mode is ``'r'``, which returns text; pass ``'rb'`` to get bytes::
+
+    from rich.progress import open
+
+    with open("data.csv") as f:
+        text = f.read()
+
+    with open("data.bin", "rb") as f:
+        data = f.read()
+
+The same reader is available on an existing :class:`~rich.progress.Progress` instance through :meth:`~rich.progress.Progress.open`. This method does not start the display, so use the instance as a context manager to show it::
+
+    import rich.progress
+
+    with rich.progress.Progress() as progress:
+        with progress.open("data.csv") as f:
+            text = f.read()
+
+Reading from a file-like object
+~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+
+Use :func:`~rich.progress.wrap_file` to show progress while reading from an open binary file-like object. The ``total`` argument is required, as a byte count. The reader does not close the wrapped file, so you must close it yourself::
+
+    import io
+    from rich.progress import wrap_file
+
+    source = io.BytesIO(b"binary data")
+    with wrap_file(source, total=11) as f:
+        data = f.read()
+    source.close()
+
+With :meth:`~rich.progress.Progress.wrap_file`, ``total`` may be omitted if the task given by ``task_id`` already has a total. Otherwise a ``ValueError`` is raised::
+
+    import io
+    import rich.progress
+
+    with rich.progress.Progress() as progress:
+        task_id = progress.add_task("Reading", total=11)
+        reader = progress.wrap_file(io.BytesIO(b"binary data"), task_id=task_id)
+        data = reader.read()
+
